arXiv:2510.02520v1 [cs.LG] 02 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xikun Huang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tianyu Ruan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Chihao Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shihua Zhang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Xikun Huang, Tianyu Ruan, Chihao Zhang and Shihua Zhang* are with the State Key Laboratory of Mathematical Sciences, Academy of Mathematics and Systems Science, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100190, China, and School of Mathematics Sciences, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China. Shihua Zhang is also with the Key Laboratory of Systems Health Science of Zhejiang Province, School of Life Science, Hangzhou Institute for Advanced Study, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Hangzhou 310024, China
<br class="ltx_break">Xikun Huang and Tianyu Ruan contributed equally to this work.
<br class="ltx_break">*To whom correspondence should be addressed. Email: zsh@amss.ac.cn.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Graph generation is a fundamental task with wide applications in modeling complex systems. Although existing methods align the spectrum or degree profile of the target graph, they often ignore the geometry induced by eigenvectors and the global structure of the graph. In this work, we propose Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching (SFMG), a novel framework that uses spectral eigenmaps to embed both input and target graphs into continuous Riemannian manifolds. We then define geodesic flows between embeddings and match distributions along these flows to generate output graphs. Our method yields several advantages: (i) captures geometric structure beyond eigenvalues, (ii) supports flexible generation of diverse graphs, and (iii) scales efficiently. Empirically, SFMG matches the performance of state-of-the-art approaches on graphlet, degree, and spectral metrics across diverse benchmarks. In particular, it achieves up to 30× speedup over diffusion-based models, offering a substantial advantage in scalability and training efficiency. We also demonstrate its ability to generalize to unseen graph scales. Overall, SFMG provides a new approach to graph synthesis by integrating spectral geometry with flow matching.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Graph generation, Spectral decomposition, Flow matching, Stiefel manifold, Geodesic flow, Deep learning

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">G
raphs are powerful tools for representing and analyzing relationships between entities in various domains. Graph generation is a critical yet challenging problem with broad applications such as combinatorial optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, drug discovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and inverse protein folding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. This problem has garnered significant attention, leading to the development of various methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Deep learning-based graph generation methods are often inspired by well-known generative models designed for regular data structures, such as images or sequences, to the irregular structure of graphs. For example, GraphVAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> extends variational autoencoders (VAEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> by jointly encoding nodes and adjacency matrices into a continuous latent space, allowing the model to generate new graphs in one shot. However, the method incurs high computational costs when applied to larger graphs and struggles to guarantee the validity of generated graphs. In contrast, GraphRNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> generates graphs autoregressively in an efficient manner. However, this approach is sensitive to permutations and prone to overfitting. SPECTRE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, on the other hand, built on generative adversarial networks (GANs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, faces the same training difficulties commonly associated with GANs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> have achieved significant success. These models utilize neural networks to learn and simulate reverse-time stochastic differential equations (SDEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and transform white noise into the data distribution. Diffusion models demonstrate the advanced capability to model complex dependencies and learn distributions. Several methods have been developed based on this.
For example, GDSS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> learns graph structures by modeling the distribution of the adjacency matrices.
However, directly adding isotropic Gaussian noise to the adjacency matrix is suboptimal, as it violates the discrete nature of adjacency matrices and may lead to invalid graph structures. To address this issue, GSDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> performs an eigendecomposition of the adjacency matrix and employs a diffusion model to learn the distribution of the eigenvalues while ignoring the eigenvectors. This approach achieves high generation quality with low computational cost. However, the omission of eigenvectors limits its ability to capture the graph structure fully. GGSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> extends this framework by applying a diffusion model to learn the distribution of eigenvectors in Euclidean space, but it neglects the intrinsic geometric constraints. Modeling the distribution of eigenvectors remains a fundamental challenge due to the orthogonal constraints, which render standard diffusion models inapplicable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As an alternative to diffusion models, flow matching models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> transform white noise into data distributions via ordinary differential equations (ODEs). These models offer comparable performance to diffusion models while being more efficient and easy to implement, especially in geometric applications. Given these advantages, flow matching emerges as a promising framework for graph generation. However, implementing these advanced methods for graph generation is nontrivial due to several inherent challenges:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sparse topological structure</span>: Graph topological structures are represented by adjacency matrices, which are often sparse. However, generative models, mainly designed for Euclidean space and continuous modeling, frequently struggle to capture this sparse structure, potentially leading to invalid graphs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-uniqueness of graph representations</span>: Graphs admit multiple representations, such as adjacency matrices, Laplacian matrix decompositions, or hyperbolic space embeddings. Different permutations in the adjacency matrix yield diverse representations, complicating the learning process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning distribution in representation space</span>: Graph learning faces the inherent challenge of limited data, especially compared to domains like images and sequences.
This drives the need for models that efficiently model distributions in high-dimensional spaces.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Addressing the first two challenges requires developing natural and effective representations for discrete graph structures. The third challenge demands efficient algorithms to model distributions in the chosen representation space. Here, we propose a spectral flow matching model for graph generation (SFMG). SFMG leverages spectral decomposition to derive compact graph representations, accommodating sparsity and resolving the non-uniqueness of graph representations by focusing on eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Furthermore, SFMG employs flow matching to enhance the effectiveness of distribution learning. It fully utilizes the geometric structure of the spectral space, thereby overcoming the challenges posed by limited graph data. This also offers superior generation efficiency compared to diffusion models. The key contribution of SFMG is its ability to model both eigenvalue and eigenvector distributions with eigenvectors modeled directly on the Stiefel manifold. To our knowledge, SFMG is the first method to achieve this. SFMG provides a more complete and expressive representation of graph structures by learning eigenvector distributions on manifolds. SFMG consists of three flow-matching models designed to generate (1) the first <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues, (2) their corresponding eigenvectors, and (3) reconstruct the adjacency matrix from spectral information while generating node features (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2510.02520v1/figures/pipeline.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:495/169;" width="495" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Illustration of SFMG. SFMG adopts flow matching in <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and geodesic flow matching in <math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> to the first <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues and eigenvectors. The last flow matching is used to recover the adjacency matrix from the generated Laplacian matrix and generate the feature matrix.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate SFMG on seven diverse graph datasets and compare it against state-of-the-art graph generative models. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that SFMG achieves high-quality learning of Laplacian eigenvalues and eigenvectors. In terms of overall graph generation, our method shows superior performance to existing approaches across different graph scales and applications as measured by diverse metrics. Notably, SFMG achieves a 30× speedup over diffusion-based methods—avoiding their scalability issues on large graphs—while matching state-of-the-art autoregressive models in structural fidelity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph Generative Models</span>.
Graph generative models are typically categorized as autoregressive or one-shot. Autoregressive models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> construct graphs sequentially by adding nodes and edges, allowing for constraint integration but suffering from limitations related to node ordering. In contrast, one-shot models generate the entire graph in a single step, naturally supporting permutation invariance or equivariance. These include variational autoencoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, GANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, normalizing flows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, with diffusion models recently achieving state-of-the-art performance via iterative noise-to-data transformations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spectral-based Graph Generation</span>. Spectral information—eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a graph’s adjacency or Laplacian matrix—encodes key structural properties such as connectivity and clustering. Leveraging this, SPECTRE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> addresses the limitations of one-shot generative models by first generating dominant spectral components and then constructing graphs to match them. However, SPECTRE relies on three GANs with complex architectures and does not directly model eigenvector distributions; instead, it refines eigenvectors from a bank of learned matrices. Alternatively, GSDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> integrates graph spectrum into the diffusion framework by applying stochastic differential equations (SDEs) to eigenvalues, while sampling eigenvectors from ground truth data. GSDM avoids eigenvector diffusion due to challenges with applying SDEs on the Stiefel manifold. GGSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, a contemporaneous work, further attempts to model the distribution of eigenvectors, but it disregards the manifold constraints. In contrast, we leverage geodesic flow matching to directly model eigenvector dynamics on the manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flow Matching on Manifolds</span>. Flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> offers an efficient alternative to diffusion models by learning straighter probability paths, with strong results in image and video generation. Importantly, it can be extended to manifold-based data. However, existing manifold methods often suffer from costly simulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, poor scalability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, or biased approximations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. To overcome these limitations, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Lipman [25]</a></cite> proposed Riemannian Flow Matching (RFM), a scalable framework using geodesic distances to define vector fields on manifolds. Building on RFM, we adapt this approach to graphs by constructing an eigenvector generator on the Stiefel manifold. A contemporaneous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> also applies flow matching on the Stiefel manifold, focusing on molecular generation with fixed moments of inertia, rather than general graph generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spectral Decomposition of Graphs</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A graph <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by a set of vertices <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of edges <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E=\{(x,y)|\,(x,y)\in V^{2},(x,y)\in E\iff(y,x)\in E)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\{(x,y)|\,(x,y)\in V^{2},(x,y)\in E\iff(y,x)\in E)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating two vertices are connected or not. The adjacency matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of the graph <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-order square matrix defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle A_{ij}=\left\{\begin{aligned} 1,&amp;&{\rm if}\,(v_{i},v_{j})\in E\\
0,&amp;&{\rm if}\,(v_{i},v_{j})\notin E\end{aligned}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{ij}=\left\{\begin{aligned} 1,&amp;&amp;{\rm if}\,(v_{i},v_{j})\in E\\
0,&amp;&amp;{\rm if}\,(v_{i},v_{j})\notin E\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The degree of a vertex is defined as the number of edges connected to it (or the sum of the weights of those edges), which is denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm deg}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm deg}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The degree matrix is an <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-order matrix defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{ij}=\left\{\begin{aligned} &{\rm deg}(v_{i}),&amp;&{\rm if}\,i=j\\
&amp;0,&amp;&{\rm if}\,i\neq j\end{aligned}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{ij}=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;{\rm deg}(v_{i}),&amp;&amp;{\rm if}\,i=j\\
&amp;0,&amp;&amp;{\rm if}\,i\neq j\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The normalized Laplacian matrix is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=I-D^{-\frac{1}{2}}AD^{-\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=I-D^{-\frac{1}{2}}AD^{-\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its eigendecomposition yields a diagonal matrix of eigenvalues and an orthogonal matrix of eigenvectors, capturing key structural features of the graph. These spectral components provide intrinsic node coordinates and support metrics such as commute time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and biharmonic distances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Thus, the Laplacian and its spectrum offer a natural, intrinsic graph representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Orthogonal Matrices and Stiefel Manifolds</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The set of orthogonal matrices is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(n)={P\in M_{n}:,P^{T}P=I_{n}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(n)={P\in M_{n}:,P^{T}P=I_{n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which forms a Riemannian manifold of dimension <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n(n-1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, embedded in the Euclidean space <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its exponential map coincides with the standard matrix exponential:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exp(Q)=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\frac{Q^{i}}{i!}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exp(Q)=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\frac{Q^{i}}{i!},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> grows, the dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math> increases quadratically, posing computational challenges. To mitigate this, we adopt the Stiefel manifold <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, a lower-dimensional submanifold of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})=\{U\in M_{n\times k}:\,U^{T}U=I_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})=\{U\in M_{n\times k}:\,U^{T}U=I_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">This manifold has a dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nk-\frac{k(k+1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nk-\frac{k(k+1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which grows linearly with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The exponential map in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm Exp}:T\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})\to\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm Exp}:T\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})\to\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm Exp}{(U,v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm Exp}{(U,v)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(U,\,v)\exp\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;-S(0)\\
I&amp;A\end{pmatrix}I_{2p,p}e^{-At}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(U,\,v)\exp\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;-S(0)\\
I&amp;A\end{pmatrix}I_{2p,p}e^{-At}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n}),\,v\in T_{U}\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n}),\,v\in T_{U}\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=U^{T}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=U^{T}v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(0)=v^{T}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(0)=v^{T}v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{2p,p}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{p}\\
0\end{pmatrix}_{2p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{2p,p}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{p}\\
0\end{pmatrix}_{2p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Basic properties of Stiefel manifolds are introduced in Appendices <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Key Properties of Stiefel Manifold 𝕍_𝑘⁢(ℝ^𝑛) ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flow Matching</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an vector field <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> on a Riemannian manifold <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> that depends on time <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the flow mapping <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the solution of the following ordinary differential equation (ODE):</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{t}(x_{0})=x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{t}(x_{0})=x(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm where}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>where</mi><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm where}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{dx}{dt}=u_{t}(x),\,x(0)=x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{dx}{dt}=u_{t}(x),\,x(0)=x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Given an initial distribution, which is usually a white noise, on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with a density function <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the push-forward measure <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{t}(\cdot)]_{*}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{t}(\cdot)]_{*}p</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the relationship between <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the continuity equation:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}p_{t}=-{\rm div}(u_{t}p_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}p_{t}=-{\rm div}(u_{t}p_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The purpose of flow matching is to fit a designed vector field <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> by a neural network <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}(t,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}(t,x)</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies the condition:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{0}(x)=p(x),\quad p_{1}(x)=q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{0}(x)=p(x),\quad p_{1}(x)=q(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the density function of the desired measure. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al. [15]</a></cite> derived such a vector field <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> by firstly designing conditional vector fields <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (or equivalently design a conditional flow mapping <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the flow mapping of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>). Denoting the push-forward measure <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})_{*}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t}(\cdot|x_{1})_{*}p</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional flow mapping satisfies:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{0}(x|x_{1})=p(x),\quad p_{1}(x|x_{1})\approx\delta_{x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{0}(x|x_{1})=p(x),\quad p_{1}(x|x_{1})\approx\delta_{x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Dirac distribution centered at <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}(x)=\int_{\mathcal{M}}u_{t}(x|x_{1})\frac{p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})}{p_{t}(x)}\,d\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}(x)=\int_{\mathcal{M}}u_{t}(x|x_{1})\frac{p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})}{p_{t}(x)}\,d\omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(x)=\int_{\mathcal{M}}p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})\,d\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(x)=\int_{\mathcal{M}}p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})\,d\omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.5" class="ltx_p">More importantly, using conditional vector fields or conditional flow mapping as supervision is sufficient for fitting <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, we optimize the following loss function to learn the vector field <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}(t,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}(t,x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{t,q(x_{1}),p(x_{0})}{\left\lVert V_{\theta}(t,x_{t})-\dot{x}_{t}\right\rVert^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{t,q(x_{1}),p(x_{0})}{\left\lVert V_{\theta}(t,x_{t})-\dot{x}_{t}\right\rVert^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\psi_{t}(x_{0}|x_{1})=u_{t}(x_{t}|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\psi_{t}(x_{0}|x_{1})=u_{t}(x_{t}|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SFMG comprises three flow-matching models (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The first two model the generation of eigenvalues <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda=(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding eigenvector matrix <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})\subset\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})\subset\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we learn vector fields that transport white noise to the data distribution:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;d\Lambda=V_{1,\theta}(\Lambda,t)\,dt\\
&amp;dU=V_{2,\theta}(U,\Lambda|_{t=1},t)\,dt\end{aligned}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;d\Lambda=V_{1,\theta}(\Lambda,t)\,dt\\
&amp;dU=V_{2,\theta}(U,\Lambda|_{t=1},t)\,dt\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2,\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2,\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is conditioned on the eigenvalues. While a full Laplacian requires all <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> eigencomponents, we approximate it using the top <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting matrix <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}=U\,{\rm diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k})\,U^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}=U\,{\rm diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k})\,U^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a valid Laplacian. To bridge this gap, we use the third flow matching model to transform it into a valid adjacency matrix and further matching the data distribution.:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle dL=V_{3,\theta}(L,t)\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle dL=V_{3,\theta}(L,t)\,dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For graphs with node features, we jointly model the distributions of adjacency and feature matrices <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> via:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;dL=V_{3,\theta}(L,F,t)\,dt\\
&amp;dF=V_{3,\theta}(L,F,t)\,dt\end{aligned}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;dL=V_{3,\theta}(L,F,t)\,dt\\
&amp;dF=V_{3,\theta}(L,F,t)\,dt\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SFMG proceeds by first performing eigendecomposition of the normalized Laplacian:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L=P\cdot{\rm diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n})\cdot P^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L=P\cdot{\rm diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n})\cdot P^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This maps each graph to <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n)\times\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n)\times\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For efficiency, we retain only the top <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors and eigenvalues. Two flow matching models are then used to learn the distribution of the first <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues in <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding eigenvectors in <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioned on the eigenvalues. The third flow model reconstructs the adjacency matrix and learns node features from the generated eigenvalues <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and eigenvectors <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, symmetry and rounding are applied to ensure valid adjacency matrices (Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>). The following subsections describe the design and implementation of these models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conditional Flow Mapping Design</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As introduced in section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Flow Matching ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>, we only need to design conditional flow mapping to implement flow matching in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Path in Euclidean space</span>
Euclidean space possesses a plane structure, allowing us to apply the simplest linear interpolation to conditional flow mapping:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi_{t}(x_{0}|x_{1})=x_{t}=x_{0}+t(x_{1}-x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi_{t}(x_{0}|x_{1})=x_{t}=x_{0}+t(x_{1}-x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which corresponds to the flow of (conditionally) optimal transport:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\psi(x_{0}|x_{1})=x_{1}-x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\psi(x_{0}|x_{1})=x_{1}-x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Path in Stiefel manifolds</span>.
The exponential map naturally defines geodesics in <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are introduced in section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Orthogonal Matrices and Stiefel Manifolds ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>. By <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Exp}(U,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Exp}(U,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and its inverse <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Log}(U,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Log}(U,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, we derive the geodesic interpolation between two points on <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. This interpolation is used to define conditional flow mapping in <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, as designed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Lipman [25]</a></cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi_{t}(U_{0}|U_{1})=x_{t}:={\rm Exp}{\big(U_{0},t\cdot{\rm Log}(U_{0},U_{1})\big)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi_{t}(U_{0}|U_{1})=x_{t}:={\rm Exp}{\big(U_{0},t\cdot{\rm Log}(U_{0},U_{1})\big)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Its corresponding conditional vector field is (Appendices <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Geodesics and the conditional vector field ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}(\cdot|U_{1})=\frac{d}{dt}\psi_{t}(U_{0}|U_{1})={\rm Log}{(U_{t},U_{1})}\frac{\|{\rm Log}{(U_{0},U_{1})}\|}{\|{\rm Log}{(U_{t},U_{1})}\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}(\cdot|U_{1})=\frac{d}{dt}\psi_{t}(U_{0}|U_{1})={\rm Log}{(U_{t},U_{1})}\frac{\|{\rm Log}{(U_{0},U_{1})}\|}{\|{\rm Log}{(U_{t},U_{1})}\|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">In practice, we compute the logarithm map of Stiefel manifolds using the algorithm proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zimmermann and Hüper [30]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Network Structure and Vector Field Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within the SFMG framework, we adopt task-specific architectures to learn vector fields <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. For graph datasets, we use a ResNet-style network composed of residual blocks:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm Block}_{\theta}(x)=\sigma\left(LN_{1}\circ W_{2}\circ\sigma\circ LN_{2}\circ W_{1}(x)+x\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Block</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm Block}_{\theta}(x)=\sigma\left(LN_{1}\circ W_{2}\circ\sigma\circ LN_{2}\circ W_{1}(x)+x\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LN_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LN_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LN_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LN_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are layer normalization layers, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are linear maps, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denotes ReLU. For molecular data, we follow the architecture used in GDSS.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the tangent space of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, we directly use the network to learn vector fields in Euclidean space. In contrast, the tangent spaces of the Stiefel manifold <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are nontrivial, and the raw network output <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> may not lie on a valid tangent plane. To ensure validity, we project <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> onto the tangent space at point <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> using the projection operator:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{2,\theta}(Y,t)=\pi_{T}(Z,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{2,\theta}(Y,t)=\pi_{T}(Z,Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Y\frac{Y^{T}Z-Z^{T}Y}{2}+(1-YY^{T})Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Y\frac{Y^{T}Z-Z^{T}Y}{2}+(1-YY^{T})Z</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">as detailed in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Key Properties of Stiefel Manifold 𝕍_𝑘⁢(ℝ^𝑛) ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. This guarantees that the learned vector field respects the geometric structure of the Stiefel manifold.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Training and Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We learn conditional geodesic flows on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the loss:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell">
<div id="S4.Ex27.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:13.5pt;vertical-align:-4.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(22.6pt,-0.9pt) scale(1.15099428746431,1.15099428746431) ;">
<p id="S4.Ex27.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{RCFM}=\mathbb{E}_{t,q(x_{1}),p(x_{0})}\left\|V_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-{\rm Log}(x_{t},x_{1})\frac{\|{\rm Log}(x_{0},x_{1})\|}{\|{\rm Log}(x_{t},x_{1})\|}\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{RCFM}=\mathbb{E}_{t,q(x_{1}),p(x_{0})}\left\|V_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-{\rm Log}(x_{t},x_{1})\frac{\|{\rm Log}(x_{0},x_{1})\|}{\|{\rm Log}(x_{t},x_{1})\|}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Log}(x_{0},x_{1})=x_{1}-x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Log}(x_{0},x_{1})=x_{1}-x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in Euclidean space, and in <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, it denotes the inverse of the exponential map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training</span>.
For the eigenvector generator, we condition the vector field on the first <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> true eigenvalues. After training the first two flow models, we generate Laplacian matrices <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are then transformed by a third model into adjacency matrices in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and used to learn the feature matrix distribution. Training follows the method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-C Training and Generation ‣ IV Method ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,<a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Training and Generation ‣ IV Method ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,<a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-C Training and Generation ‣ IV Method ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             1. Sample <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim U[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim U[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Sample <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},\Lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},\Lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from data (first <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors and eigenvalues).
Sample <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from white noise on the Stiefel manifold.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             2. Compute the flow matching loss:

<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\left\|V_{\theta}(U_{t},t\mid\Lambda_{1})-{\rm Log}(U_{t},U_{1})\frac{\|{\rm Log}(U_{0},U_{1})\|}{\|{\rm Log}(U_{t},U_{1})\|}\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\left\|V_{\theta}(U_{t},t\mid\Lambda_{1})-{\rm Log}(U_{t},U_{1})\frac{\|{\rm Log}(U_{0},U_{1})\|}{\|{\rm Log}(U_{t},U_{1})\|}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>Perform a single optimization step to minimize <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             3. Repeat Steps 1 and 2 until convergence.

</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Training of flow matching for eigenvectors</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<div id="alg2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             1. Sample <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim U[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim U[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Sample <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{1}=(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{1}=(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> from the data (first <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues).
Sample <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg2.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,I_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,I_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> (white noise).

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             2. Compute the flow matching loss:

<table id="S4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\left\|V_{\theta}(\Lambda_{t},t)-\frac{\|\Lambda_{1}-\Lambda_{0}\|}{\|\Lambda_{1}-\Lambda_{t}\|}(\Lambda_{1}-\Lambda_{t})\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\left\|V_{\theta}(\Lambda_{t},t)-\frac{\|\Lambda_{1}-\Lambda_{0}\|}{\|\Lambda_{1}-\Lambda_{t}\|}(\Lambda_{1}-\Lambda_{t})\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>Perform a single optimization step to minimize <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             3. Repeat Steps 1 and 2 until convergence.

</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Training of flow matching for eigenvalues</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<div id="alg3.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             1. Sample <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim U[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim U[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg3.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from data of the adjacency matrix and <math id="alg3.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}=U{\rm diag}(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{k})U^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}=U{\rm diag}(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{k})U^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg3.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg3.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> are generated by the eigenvector generator and eigenvalue generator respectively.
</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             2. Perform optimization on the loss function

<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|V_{\theta}(L_{t},t)-\frac{\|L_{1}-L_{0}\|}{{\|L_{1}-L_{t}}\|}(L_{1}-L_{t})\right\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|V_{\theta}(L_{t},t)-\frac{\|L_{1}-L_{0}\|}{{\|L_{1}-L_{t}}\|}(L_{1}-L_{t})\right\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>with a single step.
Repeat Steps 1 and 2 until convergence.

</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Training of flow matching for the postprocess</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="alg4" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<div id="alg4.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg4.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             1. Sample <math id="alg4.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and apply the Euler method for <math id="alg4.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="alg4.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg4.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             2. Sample <math id="alg4.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly from <math id="alg4.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and apply the Euler method for <math id="alg4.l2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{2,\theta}(\cdot\mid\Lambda_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2,\theta}(\cdot\mid\Lambda_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="alg4.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg4.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             3. Construct the initial adjacency matrix

<table id="S4.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}=U_{1}\,\text{diag}(\Lambda_{1})\,U_{1}^{T}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}=U_{1}\,\text{diag}(\Lambda_{1})\,U_{1}^{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>sample <math id="alg4.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{n\times m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{n\times m})</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply the Euler method for <math id="alg4.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{3,\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{3,\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="alg4.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg4.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg4.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             4. Apply simple post-processing to <math id="alg4.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg4.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if needed.

</div>
<div id="alg4.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg4.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return</span> adjacency matrix <math id="alg4.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and feature matrix <math id="alg4.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 4</span> </span> Sampling from SFMG</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generation</span>.
Sample generation proceeds in three stages: (1) sampling eigenvalues in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; (2) generating eigenvectors in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on them; and (3) constructing adjacency matrices from the resulting Laplacian <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (Algorithm <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ IV-C Training and Generation ‣ IV Method ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). These steps involve solving ODEs. We adopt the Euler method with step size <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, combined with manifold exponential mapping:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t+1}={\rm Exp}(x_{t},\epsilon\cdot V_{t}(x_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t+1}={\rm Exp}(x_{t},\epsilon\cdot V_{t}(x_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Exp}(x,v)=x+v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Exp}(x,v)=x+v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is defined for <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Conditional Flow Mapping Design ‣ IV Method ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a>. Empirically, we find the generation quality robust to <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and set it to 0.01 in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate graph generation performance, we use a diverse set of datasets: six general graph benchmarks (Ego-Small, Community-Small, Planar, Enzymes, SBM, and Grid) and the molecular dataset QM9. These widely used datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> cover a broad range of graph structures, enabling a comprehensive assessment of model generalization and adaptability (see Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A Datasets ‣ V Experiments ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>). We follow standard practice by splitting each dataset into 80% training and 20% test sets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Statistics of graph datasets used in this study.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Name</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">#Graphs</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">#Nodes</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Ego-Small</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">200</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">[4, 18]</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Community-Small</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">100</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">[12, 20]</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Planar</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">200</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">64</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Enzymes</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">587</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">[10, 125]</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SBM</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">200</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">[44, 192]</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Grid</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">100</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">[100, 400]</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess graph generation quality by comparing statistical distributions of key properties between generated and test graphs. Specifically, we consider:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Node degree (Deg.)</span> and <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">clustering coefficient (Clus.)</span>: local graph statistics;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Orbit count (Orbit)</span>: counts of 4-node subgraph motifs capturing higher-order structures;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normalized Laplacian eigenvalues (Spec.)</span>: global graph spectral information.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We quantify differences using <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, defined as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex33X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex33X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MMD}(\mathbb{S}_{g},\mathbb{S}_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MMD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MMD}(\mathbb{S}_{g},\mathbb{S}_{r})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex33X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{m^{2}}\sum_{i,j=1}^{m}k(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{r},\mathbf{x}_{j}^{r})+\frac{1}{n^{2}}\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}k(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{g},\mathbf{x}_{j}^{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>g</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>g</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{m^{2}}\sum_{i,j=1}^{m}k(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{r},\mathbf{x}_{j}^{r})+\frac{1}{n^{2}}\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}k(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{g},\mathbf{x}_{j}^{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex33Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex33Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\frac{2}{nm}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}k(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{g},\mathbf{x}_{j}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\frac{2}{nm}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}k(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{g},\mathbf{x}_{j}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a kernel function; we use an RBF kernel with Earth Mover’s Distance or total variation distance for efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To measure diversity, we report:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniqueness (Uni.)</span>: fraction of generated graphs belonging to unique isomorphism classes;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novelty (Nov.)</span>: fraction of generated graphs not present in the training set.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validity</span> is assessed for Planar and SBM graphs:
a valid Planar graph is connected and planar; a valid SBM graph has 2–5 communities with 20–40 nodes per community, and estimated parameters match the original with probability <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ V-F1 Graph Benchmarks ‣ V-F Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> reports validity, uniqueness, and novelty results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For QM9 molecules, we follow GDSS and additionally compute <span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fréchet ChemNet Distance (FCD)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, NSPDK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, and MMD to assess chemical and structural fidelity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Selection of <math id="S5.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the Stiefel Manifold</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the configuration proposed in SPECTRE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> to select the hyperparameter <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the Stiefel manifold <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{k}(\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, aiming to ensure both improved performance and methodological fairness.
The choice of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is guided by three considerations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(i) <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Computational cost</em>. Large <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases memory and training load, whereas using <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> alleviates overhead since the complexity of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(n)</annotation></semantics></math> grows quadratically with <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, while that of the Stiefel manifold grows linearly;</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) <em id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learning capacity</em>. Empirical results show that optimization directly on <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(n)</annotation></semantics></math> often performs poorly, while the Stiefel manifold provides a more suitable geometry for high-dimensional features; and</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(iii) <em id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data characteristics</em>. Spectral theory indicates that small eigenvalues encode global structure (e.g., connectivity, clustering), so a modest <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is often sufficient, though larger graphs may require larger values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> can be initialized following prior work (e.g., spectral decomposition with GANs to learn the first <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors) and refined with simple search strategies such as binary search when necessary.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Performance evaluation of SFMG and the competing methods on the Ego-Small and Community-Small datasets, respectively.Metrics include node degree (Deg.), clustering coefficient (Clus.), orbit counts (Orbit), and Laplacian spectra (Spec.), all measured by MMD. “Ratio” is the average MMD ratio (lower is better), and “Uniq. &amp; Nov.” measure generation diversity.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:70.1pt;vertical-align:-33.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-158.2pt,32.1pt) scale(0.52160780071494,0.52160780071494) ;">
