<section id="S5.SS6.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS6.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-F</span>4 </span>Efficiency Analysis</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the average time required to generate ten graphs using
SPECTRE (GAN-based), GDSS (diffusion-based), and SFMG (flow matching-based).
As reported in Table <a href="#S5.T8" title="TABLE VIII ‣ V-F4 Efficiency Analysis ‣ V-F Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a>, SPECTRE is the fastest (0.014–0.302s) due to the inherent efficiency of GAN sampling.
GDSS is the slowest (18.244–102.870s), reflecting the iterative computational overhead of diffusion-based methods.
SFMG, our flow matching-based approach, strikes a practical balance: its generation times (0.140–0.912s) are over <math id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="100\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than GDSS, yet slightly slower than SPECTRE.
Importantly, despite this modest increase in time compared to GANs, SFMG delivers superior graph generation quality, demonstrating that flow matching provides both high fidelity and scalable performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE VIII: </span>Time costs (seconds) of three different methods for generating ten graphs across six datasets.</figcaption>
