<td id="A3.T13.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T13.2.3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">75.0</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A3.T12" title="TABLE XII ‣ C-A Exclusion of GSDM as a Baseline. ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XII</span></a> and <a href="#A3.T13" title="TABLE XIII ‣ C-A Exclusion of GSDM as a Baseline. ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XIII</span></a>, although GSDM reports competitive graph generation scores, we ultimately excluded it as a baseline due to two primary limitations. First, it demonstrates low generation novelty: both its uniqueness and novelty metrics are notably low (approximately 50), suggesting a high degree of output redundancy and potential overfitting. Second, GSDM models only the eigenvalues while disregarding the eigenvectors, resulting in a significant loss of structural information and an incomplete spectral representation. This design choice undermines its ability to capture the full complexity of graph structures. In contrast, our method (SFMG) jointly models eigenvalues and eigenvectors, enabling richer spectral encoding and improved generalization. Supporting experimental results are presented in the subsequent sections.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Visualization of Generated Graphs</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the visualizations of graphs from the training and generated ones by SFMG for each dataset (Figures <a href="#A4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ Appendix D Visualization of Generated Graphs ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-<a href="#A4.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ Appendix D Visualization of Generated Graphs ‣ Graph Generation with Spectral Geodesic Flow Matching" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). The visualized graphs are randomly selected from both the training and generated ones. Additionally, we provide information on the number of edges (<math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>) and nodes (<math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>) for general graphs.</p>
</div>
